I'm Curtin Divina reading for reviewer  Steve Watts minecraft represented a  massive paradigm shift in games having  served as a popular proto example of  both early access release and  unstructured creation based gameplay  more than a decade later minecraft  dungeons doesn't strive toward  revolutionary but it may just use the  now-familiar trappings of its namesake  to introduce a new generation of players  to old-school tropes the dungeon crawler  is a light breezy introduction to the  genre for newcomers and a friendly  low-impact callback for veterans those  experience with games like Diablo or  torchlight already know the basic gist  you venture from a hub area into various  environments battle enemy hordes  occasionally fell some larger-than-life  boss monsters and then spend time laying  out and sorting through your new loot  like a kid will just open a pack of  baseball cards within that framework  there is some simplification in  Minecraft dungeons which helps to make  it more inviting you only have six gear  slots melee bow armor and three artifact  based abilities you won't find  specialized classes or complex skill  trees here everything is tied to your  armor sets and the level ups mostly  matter and that they determine the  quality of your loot drops that loot  rains down constantly  sometimes a sprinkle but more often a  monsoon which means you'll be constantly  swapping your weapons and armor sets  minecraft dungeons is far more precious  with its gear so it's not uncommon to  find yourself switching three or four  times in a single stage at first these  upgrades appear pretty linear replacing  a sword with a new stronger sword but  before long the enchantment system comes  into play which provides dungeons with  its strategic depth enchantments are  specialized upgrades to each of your  primary pieces of gear each level of  gives you an enchantment point to select  one from a handful passive buffs but you  earn back your spent points whenever you  salvage a piece of equipment that's  already been enchanted in the early game  when a weapon might only have one  enchantment slot and fairly vanilla  buffs  these are nice bonuses but don't  significantly impact how you approach  the game as you progress later these  instrumental and adding depth in a  difficulty ramp finding more complex  enchantment combinations can make the  difference between a brittle build or a  powerhouse that also makes the search  for new loot both thrilling and  progressively more thoughtful since you  earn your in Chapman points back for  salvaging swapping out a new piece of  gear means you can enchant it with  several upgrades from the start the late  game is a series of constant trade-offs  finding your rhythm with a new set of  abilities and buffs before exchanging  them for something completely different  while enchantments make of the combat  strategically complex  the moment-to-moment gameplay is  relatively simple most melee attacks  don't stagger opponents so there's no  weight or impact hits you'll often just  hit enemies as they hit you like a  couple of Rock'em Sock'em robots until  one of you falls down the melee weapons  are well differentiated by their tech  speed and range but that's basically all  melee battle can also feel a little  unfair when you're surrounded by mobs  and unable to move especially if you  find yourself dropped there by surprise  from a jump pad sometimes the simplicity  of the combat can get in its own way you  can pick up TNT boxes as random drops  and carry them around with you for when  you need a quick area of effect attack  but the throw command is set to the same  button as the ranged attack that makes  it impossible to fire an arrow without  getting rid of the TNT boxes you may  have been saving and the two don't  really serve the same function this just  means you have to sacrifice your a OE  availability any time you need the  precision of an arrow or anytime you  forget in the heat of the moment which  happened to me more times than I can  count  minecraft dungeons is short on its  default difficulty setting completing  default opens up adventure mode with  higher-level enemies in better loot and  completing adventure opens apocalypse  the game is built around relatively  short self-contained repeatable stages  each with difficulty sliders adventure  mode starts to roll out gear with more  powerful abilities that both provides  more incentive to keep hunting better  loot on the higher difficulty and gives  you something to do with excess and  chapman points this emphasis on finding  Luke comes at the expense of the in-game  economy because despite finding gems  frequently throughout dungeons there  just isn't much use for them  you can spend them to roll  piece of gear your camp but it hardly  ever stacks up to the gear you've  already found and turning unwanted gear  to salvage only gets you a fraction of  your money back as a result it's not  really worth it and there's nothing else  to spend your hard-won gems on the  stages themselves are minecraft accurate  carrying over an aesthetic made of parts  that are clearly recognizable to anyone  who has spent time in the block building  game the minecraft spirit is also  carried through mobs themselves classic  enemies like creepers or skeletons are  among the first you'll see and sometimes  the stage will darken for randomized  visit from the Enderman as a mini-boss  encounter dungeons also find ways to  create its own bits of Minecraft  identity like the addition of the  adorable key golem the co-op works  without a hitch offering an easy way to  join up with friends or guide less  experienced players through tough  challenges gear can be concentrated in  different directions with emphasis on  elements like healing or damage  mitigation which could theoretically  allow a very well-coordinated team  replicate the standard tank healer DPS  model realistically though a team will  be compromised of several single-player  builds and simply having partners work  to help mitigate some of the crowd  control issues that exist when going  solo a few smart inclusions like  color-coded loot and quick teleport tool  make it simple to stay together and let  everyone enjoy their winnings it is odd  though that starting an offline game  still functions like a connected one  including the inability to pause  minecraft dungeons is missing  conspicuous parts of what gave the  namesake its identity most noticeably  breaking through walls to explore  underground caverns and using the found  materials to craft but maybe because of  that dungeons shows how flexible the  franchise has become rather than shift  our expectations of what games can be  it's baking on its own popularity to  introduce players to a classic genre  they might not have tried before and  serve as a short but sweet treat for  looter vets  [Music]  you
 

